          "source": "thm-locally-integrable-functions-embed-in-distributions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. The map $f\\mapsto u_f$, $u_f(\\varphi)=\\int_\\Omega f\\varphi$, is a complex-linear injection from $L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\Omega)$ modulo almost-everywhere equality into $\\mathcal D'(\\Omega)$. It is continuous from the local $L^1$ topology, generated by $f\\mapsto\\int_K|f|$ for compact $K\\subseteq\\Omega$, to the strong distribution topology. In particular local $L^1$ convergence implies strong distribution convergence. The continuity estimate itself is choice-free; Countable Choice is used in the cited $L^1$ approximate-identity theorem proving injectivity.",
