          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice and let $n\\ge2$. Fix $R>0$, and let\n$f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ be Lebesgue measurable, integrable, and compactly\nsupported, with $\\|f\\|_1:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}|f(y)|\\,dy<\\infty$, and zero outside the open\nEuclidean ball $B_R(0)$. Put\n$M:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}f(y)\\,dy$ and let $\\omega_{n-1}$ be the sphere-area\nnormalization in the definition of $\\Phi$.\n\nFor every $x\\in\\mathbb R^n$ with $r:=|x|>2R$, the Newtonian integral\n$Nf(x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}\\Phi(x-y)f(y)\\,dy$ is absolutely finite. If $n\\ge3$,\nthen\n$$\n\\left|Nf(x)-\\frac{M r^{2-n}}{(n-2)\\omega_{n-1}}\\right| \\le \\frac{2^{n-1}R}{\\omega_{n-1}}\\|f\\|_1 r^{1-n}.\n$$\nIf $n=2$, then\n$$\n\\left|Nf(x)+\\frac{M}{2\\pi}\\log r\\right| \\le \\frac{R}{\\pi}\\|f\\|_1 r^{-1}.\n$$\nThe constants are independent of the direction of $x$. In particular, when\n$M=0$ these are the respective improved orders\n$Nf(x)=O(r^{1-n})$ for $n\\ge3$ and $Nf(x)=O(r^{-1})$ for $n=2$.",
