          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, [[def-countable-choice]]). Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be open and let $(S,\\Omega_-,\\Omega_+,n)$ be a two-sided presentation of a compact embedded $C^2$ interface in $\\Omega$ in the sense of [[def-em-surface-distribution]]: $\\Omega\\setminus S=\\Omega_-\\cup\\Omega_+$ are the two open sides and $n:S\\to\\mathbb R^3$ is the continuous unit normal pointing from $\\Omega_-$ to $\\Omega_+$. Let $F:\\Omega\\setminus S\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be of class $C^1$ on $\\Omega_-$ and on $\\Omega_+$ separately, with one-sided $C^1$ traces on $S$: $F$ and each first partial derivative $\\partial_iF$ extend continuously from each side to $S$, and the traces $F_+,F_-\\in C^0(S;\\mathbb R^3)$ give the jump $[F]=F_+-F_-$.\n\nLet $\\{\\partial_iF_j\\}$, $\\{\\operatorname{div}F\\}$ and $\\{\\operatorname{curl}F\\}$ denote the locally integrable fields equal on $\\Omega_\\pm$ to the corresponding classical derivative of $F$; these are regular distributions ([[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function]]). The slice-chart definition makes $S$ a hypersurface without manifold boundary, so no edge is part of these data. Then, in the distributional sense,\n\n$$\\partial_iF_j=\\{\\partial_iF_j\\}+n_i\\,[F_j]\\,\\delta_S\\qquad(i,j\\in\\{1,2,3\\}),$$\n\nand consequently\n\n$$\\operatorname{div}F=\\{\\operatorname{div}F\\}_{\\mathrm{piecewise}}+n\\cdot[F]\\,\\delta_S,\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}F=\\{\\operatorname{curl}F\\}_{\\mathrm{piecewise}}+n\\times[F]\\,\\delta_S,$$\n\nas distributions on $\\Omega$: their pairings agree for every $\\psi\\in\\mathcal D(\\Omega)$. Here $\\delta_S$ is the surface distribution of [[def-em-surface-distribution]], $n\\cdot[F]=\\sum_i n_i[F_i]$, and $n\\times[F]$ is the cross product of [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]. A moving interface with $\\partial_tS\\ne0$ requires additional terms and is not covered; the stationary jump formulas may not be reused for a mo